//! The floor: the trap seam, the status split, and the whole `@export`ed C-ABI
//! surface (spec §5.7) every typed wrapper above is written on.
//!
//! It is the bottom of the module order because it names no wrapper type: an op
//! here takes a raw handle word and answers a raw status, which makes it
//! reachable from every object kind without reaching back into any of them.
//! `checked` is where a number becomes a `Result`, and `sos_handle_release` is
//! the one call every wrapper's `deinit` issues, so the owning tier's whole
//! contract is recorded here too, below every file that implements it.
//!
//! The two syscall externs live here and nowhere else, and that is what put the
//! per-op C surface here with them: an `extern "C"` declaration carries no
//! visibility modifier, so it cannot be shared with a sibling module, and two
//! modules declaring one symbol is an ambiguity error at the importer. One
//! declaration, one file, and the typed layer above calls the named seams.

import sosabi.{SosStatus, SystemOp, ProcessOp, ThreadOp, EventOp, WaiterOp,
               InterruptOp, ClockOp, TimerOp, MemoryOp, IoMemoryOp, MappingOp,
               PipeInletOp, PipeOutletOp, PipeReplyOp, PipeRequestOp,
               RELEASE_OP, MINT_OP}

// The per-arch HAL's whole surface: the one instruction in the system that
// crosses the trap boundary. Fixed arity on purpose: a varargs call across a
// trap boundary has no portable register story, so a wider op gets a wider
// fixed form beside these (`sos_syscall3` is that form), not an ellipsis.
extern "C" {
    func sos_syscall1(handle: UInt, op: UInt, arg0: UInt) -> UInt
    func sos_syscall3(handle: UInt, op: UInt, arg0: UInt, arg1: UInt, arg2: UInt,
                      value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) -> UInt
}

// =============================================================================
// Errors
// =============================================================================

// How a syscall reports what happened is `sosabi`'s `SosStatus` (spec §5.7),
// the same declaration the kernel's dispatch returns, so the two halves of the
// contract cannot disagree about what tag 3 means. It carries its own
// `describe()`, `Printable` and `Error`. A process reaches all of it through
// the value it is handed and never imports `sosabi`: the type's own module is
// always consulted for its methods, and an enum case pattern resolves from the
// scrutinee. So userspace can interpolate a failure (`print("{}", e)`) as well
// as `match` it.
//
// It is a status, and only some statuses are errors. `checked` below is the one
// place that distinction is made, and it keeps the `Ok` case out of the error
// channel entirely.

/// Turn a raw status word into a Result. The one place a number becomes a type.
///
/// The split: `Ok` becomes `Result.Ok`, everything else becomes `Err(status)`.
/// The `Ok` case exists on the wire, where 0 has to mean something, and cannot
/// exist in an `Err`: `Err(SosStatus.Ok)`, a value that would claim a call both
/// failed and succeeded, is unconstructible downstream because this function is
/// the only constructor of the error channel.
///
/// The narrowing is guarded rather than assumed: the status register is
/// register-wide, the enum is byte-wide (its width is ABI), and an `as UInt8`
/// on a value above 255 is a panic, in a process, over a word the process did
/// not choose. A kernel compiled from this same table never returns one, so the
/// guard is unreachable and costs a compare; what it buys is that the
/// impossible case is an error value rather than a trap.
public(package) func checked(raw: UInt) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
    if raw > (UInt8.max as UInt) {
        return SosStatus.Unknown
    }
    guard let status = SosStatus.from(raw: raw as UInt8) else {
        return SosStatus.Unknown
    }
    if status.is_failure() {
        return status
    }
    return
}

/// The same split for an op that answers with a value (a created thread's
/// handle, a joined thread's exit code, a status word). `Ok` carries the value;
/// anything else is the error and the value is not read.
func checked_value(raw: UInt, value: UInt) -> Result<UInt, SosStatus> {
    try checked(raw)
    return value
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The level decode
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `WouldBlock` is an ordinary error at the typed tier, everywhere but one op. A
// transient condition rides the error channel (error plus retry), not
// `Ok(None)`: a caller that waits on the level with a `Waiter` first reaches
// these ops already knowing the answer, and an Optional in the signature would
// make every such caller unwrap for the rare refusal. `take` and `post` use
// `checked` and `call_value` like every other op.
//
// This diverges from `Channel.try_receive` on purpose. A Saw channel's
// `try_receive` is the only door onto a channel that does not block, so its
// caller is a poll loop by construction. A pipe has four doors (attach and
// wait, the fused `Call`, the parking `Resolve`, and these polls), so its
// caller has already chosen to poll by picking the door, and does not need the
// shape to keep reminding it.
//
// The one decode here that answers a level, not a poll, is `polled_ok`.
// `PipeReplyOp.Ready` asks whether a reply has arrived and answers a `Bool` on
// the Ok channel with the terminal on the error channel. "Not yet" is the
// question's whole point, so it is a real domain answer rather than a
// transient absence.

/// Decode a status word for an op that asks a level: `Ok(true)` the level is
/// up, `Ok(false)` it is not, and every other status is the error channel,
/// which is where a terminal lives (`PipeReply.ready`'s `Err(PeerClosed)`: the
/// answer can never arrive, so there is no second call to make).
public(package) func polled_ok(raw: UInt) -> Result<Bool, SosStatus> {
    // The same guarded narrowing `checked` writes, and for the same reason: the
    // status register is register-wide and the enum is byte-wide, so an `as
    // UInt8` on a word a kernel skew produced would be a panic in a process
    // over a number it did not choose.
    if raw > (UInt8.max as UInt) {
        return SosStatus.Unknown
    }
    guard let status = SosStatus.from(raw: raw as UInt8) else {
        return SosStatus.Unknown
    }
    match status {
        case Ok -> { return true },
        case WouldBlock -> { return false },
        case _ -> {},
    }
    return status
}

/// Issue an op that answers with a value.
///
/// `unsafe` because it names a pointer: the value register comes back through
/// an out-parameter, which is the shape that crosses a C ABI with no aggregate
/// return. The typed methods above call this and stay safe signatures; the
/// pointer never leaves this function.
public(package) func call_value(handle: UInt, op: UInt, arg0: UInt, arg1: UInt,
                                arg2: UInt) unsafe -> Result<UInt, SosStatus> {
    var value: UInt = 0
    let raw = sos_syscall3(handle, op, arg0, arg1, arg2,
                           (&var value) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
    checked_value(raw, value)
}

// =============================================================================
// The C-ABI surface (spec §5.7), for non-Saw languages
// =============================================================================
//
// Per-op functions are the supported C interface: one function per operation,
// named for it, with the arguments the operation actually takes. A C caller
// gets the same contract a Saw caller does minus the type wrapper, and still
// never writes an op number.
//
// `sos_syscall1` underneath is the raw form and is reachable, but it is the
// escape hatch, not the interface: it takes an op number, which is the thing
// this whole arrangement exists to keep out of callers.
//
// Whitelist-clean by construction: handles, ops and statuses are all integers,
// so nothing here needs a type the C ABI cannot carry.

/// Write one byte to the debug console. Requires `SystemRight.Debug`. Returns a
/// status word: 0 on success.
@export("sos_system_debug_print")
public func sos_system_debug_print(handle: UInt, byte: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, SystemOp.DebugPrint as UInt, byte)
}

/// Stop the machine with `status`. Requires `SystemRight.Shutdown`. Does not
/// return on success; returns a status word if the call was refused.
@export("sos_system_shutdown")
public func sos_system_shutdown(handle: UInt, status: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, SystemOp.Shutdown as UInt, status)
}

// The ops that answer with a value take an out-parameter for it, as the raw
// form does. A C caller writes `unsigned long h; sos_process_thread_self(p,
// &h);` and gets the same contract a Saw caller does minus the Result.

/// This process's own handle. Requires `SystemRight.ProcessSelf`.
///
/// The handle carries the Process default set intersected with `keep` (all
/// ones keeps the whole set). A caller at its handle quota gets
/// `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded`; a full handle table is `SosStatus.NoResource`.
@export("sos_system_process_self")
public func sos_system_process_self(handle: UInt, keep: UInt,
                                    value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, SystemOp.ProcessSelf as UInt, keep, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Load the image in region `image` into region `memory` and answer with a
/// handle to the new, inert process. Requires `SystemRight.ProcessCreate` on
/// the caller's System handle (processes are minted by the System).
///
/// Both arguments are Memory handles: the read-only source blob and the child's
/// destination RAM. The kernel copies each segment to its link address inside
/// `memory` and grants a stack at that region's top; the caller supplies no
/// addresses at all.
///
/// A malformed image is `SosStatus.BadImage`, a value, not a fault, because
/// image bytes are data the caller was handed rather than something it wrote. A
/// handle that names nothing, or names something that is not a Memory, is a
/// fault: the caller chose which handles to pass. A full process table or
/// handle table is `SosStatus.NoResource`, and a caller at its handle quota
/// gets `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded`.
@export("sos_system_process_create")
public func sos_system_process_create(handle: UInt, image: UInt, memory: UInt,
                                      value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, SystemOp.ProcessCreate as UInt, image, memory, 0,
                 value_out)
}

/// Give the kernel a Memory region to back the slab `kind` names.
/// Requires `SystemRight.SlabDonate`.
///
/// The region's capability is consumed on success, and no op gives a donated
/// range back. `kind` is a `SlabKind`.
///
/// A kind that is not a `SlabKind`, a handle that names nothing or names
/// something that is not a Memory, and a region the kernel cannot safely absorb
/// (one with a sibling handle, one with an installed mapping row, or a pool
/// root with pieces still on loan) are all faults: the caller minted the
/// siblings, installed the rows and cut the pieces, so each is something it
/// could have checked. A region too small to hold one slot of that kind, a
/// slab whose extent chain is already full, and a donation that would take the
/// kind past its machine-wide ceiling are `SosStatus.NoResource`, a status; the
/// donation did not happen, so the caller's handle is untouched.
@export("sos_system_slab_donate")
public func sos_system_slab_donate(handle: UInt, kind: UInt,
                                   memory: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, SystemOp.SlabDonate as UInt, kind, memory, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Bind IRQ `line` to a new Interrupt in this process. Requires
/// `SystemRight.InterruptBind`. A line this machine does not have, the timer's
/// line, or one already bound terminates the process.
@export("sos_system_interrupt_bind")
public func sos_system_interrupt_bind(handle: UInt, line: UInt,
                                      value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, SystemOp.InterruptBind as UInt, line, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Make a thread that runs in the process the handle names, ready to start.
/// Requires `ProcessRight.ThreadCreate`. `entry` may be `ENTRY_IMAGE`.
///
/// The thread is charged to the named process's Thread quota and its handle
/// goes into the caller's table. An `entry` or `stack_top` outside the named
/// process's region terminates the caller; a process that has ended is
/// `SosStatus.Revoked`, answered before that check.
@export("sos_process_thread_create")
public func sos_process_thread_create(handle: UInt, entry: UInt, stack_top: UInt,
                                      arg: UInt,
                                      value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.ThreadCreate as UInt, entry, stack_top, arg,
                 value_out)
}

/// The calling thread's own handle. Requires `ProcessRight.ThreadSelf`. A
/// handle naming another process terminates the caller.
@export("sos_process_thread_self")
public func sos_process_thread_self(handle: UInt,
                                    value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.ThreadSelf as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// End this process with `status`. Requires `ProcessRight.Exit`. Does not
/// return on success.
@export("sos_process_exit")
public func sos_process_exit(handle: UInt, status: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, ProcessOp.Exit as UInt, status)
}

/// This process's §8 status word. Requires `ProcessRight.Wait`.
@export("sos_process_get_status")
public func sos_process_get_status(handle: UInt,
                                   value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.GetStatus as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Make a created thread runnable. Requires `ThreadRight.Start`. A thread of a
/// process that has not been started terminates the caller.
@export("sos_thread_start")
public func sos_thread_start(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, ThreadOp.Start as UInt, 0)
}

/// Wait for a thread and take its exit value. Requires `ThreadRight.Join`.
@export("sos_thread_join")
public func sos_thread_join(handle: UInt,
                            value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ThreadOp.Join as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// End the calling thread with `code`. Requires `ThreadRight.Control` on the
/// caller's own handle. Does not return.
@export("sos_thread_exit")
public func sos_thread_exit(handle: UInt, code: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, ThreadOp.Exit as UInt, code)
}

/// Give the processor up. Requires `ThreadRight.Control` on the caller's own
/// handle.
@export("sos_thread_yield")
public func sos_thread_yield(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, ThreadOp.Yield as UInt, 0)
}

/// Make an Event owned by the process the handle names, with its handle in the
/// caller's table. Requires `ProcessRight.EventCreate`. `mode` is 0 for
/// bitwise-OR accumulation and 1 for saturating-sum (spec §2.4); any other
/// value terminates the caller. A process that has ended is
/// `SosStatus.Revoked`.
///
/// The C altitude writes the mode as a number, the documented difference in
/// kind the module docstring describes: a C caller has no enum to be handed, so
/// it gets the wire value.
@export("sos_process_event_create")
public func sos_process_event_create(handle: UInt, mode: UInt,
                                     value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.EventCreate as UInt, mode, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Make a Waiter owned by the process the handle names, with its handle in the
/// caller's table. Requires `ProcessRight.WaiterCreate`. A process that has
/// ended is `SosStatus.Revoked`.
@export("sos_process_waiter_create")
public func sos_process_waiter_create(handle: UInt,
                                      value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.WaiterCreate as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// End this fire and let the device interrupt again. Requires
/// `InterruptRight.Ack`. Acking one that has not fired terminates the process.
@export("sos_interrupt_ack")
public func sos_interrupt_ack(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, InterruptOp.Ack as UInt, 0)
}

/// Run a created process: mint its first thread at the recorded entry, on the
/// recorded stack, and make it runnable. Requires `ProcessRight.Start` on the
/// child's handle. The caller keeps running.
///
/// A second start terminates the caller: it is broken code, and the caller is
/// the one that started it the first time.
///
/// `boot_tag_form` says whether `boot_tag` means anything: 0 is the no-tag form
/// and puts nothing (`NO_HANDLE`) in the child's first argument register; 1
/// resolves `boot_tag` against the child's boot set and puts the child-side
/// word there. Any other value terminates the caller.
///
/// It is a flag argument rather than a reserved tag value because zero is a
/// legitimate tag (root's region ordinals start at 0) and because a tag is the
/// giver's own word, which the kernel must not reserve values out of. A tag
/// that names no given record terminates the caller: it is config the caller
/// wrote both halves of.
///
/// A child at its thread quota is `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded` and a full thread
/// slab is `SosStatus.NoResource`; on either the child stays `Created` and its
/// boot set is untouched.
///
/// The C altitude writes the form as a number (see `sos_process_event_create`'s
/// mode): a C caller has no enum to be handed. The Saw altitude offers
/// `start()` and `start(boot_tag:)`.
@export("sos_process_start")
public func sos_process_start(handle: UInt, boot_tag_form: UInt,
                              boot_tag: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.Start as UInt, boot_tag_form, boot_tag, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Give `given` to this process under `tag`: the courier op. Requires
/// `ProcessRight.Give` on the child's handle ("may place handles into this
/// child's boot set") and the universal `Transfer` right on the handle being
/// given.
///
/// It moves the handle: a fresh entry binds in the child's table carrying the
/// same kind and target and the given handle's rights intersected with `keep`
/// (all ones keeps them all), the caller's entry is unbound and its generation
/// bumped, and a record is appended to the child's boot set under `tag`. The
/// return is a status alone: the child-side word is meaningless to the caller,
/// which can call no op through it, so nothing hands it back. What crosses is
/// the tag, which the child reads at its drain and which the kernel never
/// interprets.
///
/// The giver's word is dead afterwards. Using it again is the ordinary
/// `BadHandle` fault, which is what generations are for. Typed Saw callers
/// cannot meet that: `Process.give` consumes the wrapper and disarms it before
/// the syscall.
///
/// Four ways to terminate the caller, all caller-checkable: a `given` that
/// names nothing (`BadHandle`); one without `Transfer`, or a receiver handle
/// without `Give` (`AccessDenied`); a child that has already been started,
/// since the boot set freezes at start (`BadState`); and a `tag` the child's
/// set already carries (`DuplicateKey`). A full child table or boot set is
/// `SosStatus.NoResource` and a child at its handle quota is
/// `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded`. Both are statuses, and the give did not happen:
/// the caller's handle is untouched.
@export("sos_process_give")
public func sos_process_give(handle: UInt, given: UInt, tag: UInt,
                             keep: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.Give as UInt, given, tag, keep,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Take the next handle out of the calling process's boot set, as a record
/// copied into `buf` (spec §12). Requires `ProcessRight.BootHandles`. A handle
/// naming another process terminates the caller.
///
/// `cap` is the buffer's size in bytes and must be at least
/// `boot_handle_record_bytes()`. The buffer must be word-aligned and inside
/// the caller's writable memory; a buffer that is not terminates the caller,
/// because it is a mistake only the caller could have made.
///
/// The record's layout is public: word 0 the tag (a region ordinal in root's
/// set, the launcher's tag in a child's), word 1 a `BootHandleKind`, word 2 the
/// handle. An exhausted set answers `SosStatus.Drained` and keeps answering
/// it; that is a loop's termination check, not a failure.
@export("sos_process_boot_handle_next")
public func sos_process_boot_handle_next(handle: UInt, buf: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                         cap: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.BootHandleNext as UInt, buf as UInt, cap, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Read this process's trap counters into `buf`, a copy-out record because
/// three 64-bit columns fit no value register on either profile. Requires
/// `ProcessRight.Stats`.
///
/// `cap` is the buffer's size in bytes and must be at least
/// `process_stats_record_bytes()`. The buffer must be doubleword-aligned and
/// inside the caller's writable memory; a buffer that is not terminates the
/// caller, because it is a mistake only the caller could have made.
///
/// The record's layout is public: doubleword 0 the syscall count, doubleword 1
/// the interrupt count, doubleword 2 the fault count. Which one is worth
/// asserting on is `sosabi`'s to say; in short, the first is exact for a given
/// program path, the second moves with the machine, the third is exact where a
/// fault was provoked on purpose.
///
/// The counters belong to the handle's process, not to the caller. Asking your
/// own Process handle reads your own; asking a child's reads the child's, alive
/// or dead. The call itself is counted before the answer is assembled, so a
/// self-reading already includes the trap that took it.
@export("sos_process_stats")
public func sos_process_stats(handle: UInt, buf: UnsafePointer<UInt64>,
                              cap: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.Stats as UInt, buf as UInt, cap, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Accumulate `bits` into an Event. Never blocks. Requires `EventRight.Signal`.
@export("sos_event_signal")
public func sos_event_signal(handle: UInt, bits: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, EventOp.Signal as UInt, bits)
}

/// Drain an Event's word and reset it to zero. Requires `EventRight.Receive`.
@export("sos_event_receive")
public func sos_event_receive(handle: UInt,
                              value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, EventOp.Receive as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Attach a waitable to a Waiter under `key`, which must not already be in use
/// on that Waiter. Requires `WaiterRight.Attach` on the waiter and the target
/// kind's `Wait` right on the target (`EventRight.Wait` for an Event).
///
/// It takes three arguments and answers with a status alone, and the raw forms
/// are fixed-arity by design (§5.7: no varargs across a trap boundary), so it
/// rides the three-argument form and drops the value. The scratch that catches
/// it is what makes this signature `unsafe`; the pointer never leaves the
/// function.
///
/// The third argument is a flags word: `WAITER_ADD_ONESHOT` detaches at
/// delivery, and `WAITER_ADD_CONSUMING` releases the caller's entry and hands
/// the object to the attachment. A word carrying any other bit is a `BadArg`
/// fault.
@export("sos_waiter_add")
public func sos_waiter_add(handle: UInt, target: UInt, key: UInt,
                           flags: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, WaiterOp.Add as UInt, target, key, flags,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Detach the attachment registered under `key`. Requires `WaiterRight.Attach`.
///
/// It takes the key, not the waitable's handle: detaching edits the Waiter's
/// own table and never touches the waitable, so the key is the whole of what
/// names the attachment.
@export("sos_waiter_remove")
public func sos_waiter_remove(handle: UInt, key: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, WaiterOp.Remove as UInt, key)
}

/// Block until an attached handle is ready, and copy out a record describing it
/// into `buf` (spec §2.2). Requires `WaiterRight.Wait`.
///
/// `cap` is the buffer's size in bytes and must be at least
/// `wait_record_bytes()`. The buffer must be word-aligned and inside this
/// process's writable memory; a buffer that is not terminates the process,
/// because it is a mistake only the caller could have made.
///
/// The record's layout is public: word 0 the attacher's key, word 1 a
/// `WaitTag`, word 2 the payload. A C caller reads the words; a Saw caller gets
/// them decoded into a `WaitResult` by `Waiter.wait`.
@export("sos_waiter_wait")
public func sos_waiter_wait(handle: UInt, buf: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                            cap: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, WaiterOp.Wait as UInt, buf as UInt, cap, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// This process's Clock for the domain `type` names. Requires
/// `SystemRight.ClockGet`. A `type` that is not a `ClockType` terminates the
/// process.
///
/// Each call mints a fresh handle onto the one Clock: two asks are two
/// capability instances naming one kernel-eternal object, and the caller owns
/// and releases each. A caller at its handle quota gets
/// `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded`; a full handle table is `SosStatus.NoResource`.
@export("sos_system_clock_get")
public func sos_system_clock_get(handle: UInt, type: UInt,
                                 value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, SystemOp.ClockGet as UInt, type, 0, 0, value_out)
}

// There is no stub for `SystemOp.TierGet`, nor for `SystemOp.StatsRegionMap`.
// An `@export`ed stub is a linker GC root (it lands in `llvm.used`), so it
// links into every process image whether or not anything calls it, and every
// such export grows every image: a process that calls none of the stubs here
// still carries all of them. Making the C altitude opt-in per package is a
// separate piece of work. The typed `System.tier()` and
// `System.stats_region_map()` are the surface.

/// Read the clock into `buf`, a copy-out record because a nanosecond count is
/// 64 bits and one profile's registers are not. Requires `ClockRight.Read`.
///
/// `cap` is the buffer's size in bytes and must be at least
/// `clock_now_record_bytes()`. Doubleword 0 is the reading.
@export("sos_clock_now")
public func sos_clock_now(handle: UInt, buf: UnsafePointer<UInt64>,
                          cap: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, ClockOp.Now as UInt, buf as UInt, cap, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Make a Timer bound to this Clock. Requires `ClockRight.TimerCreate`.
@export("sos_clock_timer_create")
public func sos_clock_timer_create(handle: UInt,
                                   value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ClockOp.TimerCreate as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Arm a Timer from a copy-in record at `record`: doubleword 0 the delay from
/// now, doubleword 1 the repeat interval (0 for one-shot), both in nanoseconds.
/// Requires `TimerRight.Arm`.
///
/// This is the copy-out funnel's mirror twin: the op that hands the kernel a
/// record rather than taking one. `bytes` must be exactly
/// `timer_arm_record_bytes()`; a record that is misaligned, outside this
/// process's memory, or the wrong length terminates the process, because all
/// three are mistakes only the caller could have made.
///
/// Arming an already-armed Timer replaces its schedule and clears any fires it
/// had not yet delivered. That is not an error; it is how a timeout is changed.
@export("sos_timer_arm")
public func sos_timer_arm(handle: UInt, record: UnsafePointer<UInt64>,
                          bytes: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, TimerOp.Arm as UInt, record as UInt, bytes, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Stop a Timer and discard any fires it had not delivered. Requires
/// `TimerRight.Arm`.
///
/// Disarming one that is not armed is a no-op, deliberately: a one-shot disarms
/// itself when it fires, so cancelling a timeout that has just expired is an
/// ordinary race rather than a caller error. (The opposite of
/// `sos_interrupt_ack`, whose no-fire case is a fault; see `Timer.disarm`.)
@export("sos_timer_disarm")
public func sos_timer_disarm(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, TimerOp.Disarm as UInt, 0)
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Memory, IoMemory and Mapping (spec §2.5)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Five ops across three kinds. Each memory kind has a derivation verb and an
// install verb, and the third kind is what an install produces. The verbs
// differ because the kinds do: a split consumes what it hands out and a carve
// does not.

/// Split: cut a `len`-byte piece out of a region. Requires
/// `MemoryRight.Split`.
///
/// On a pool root the piece is served first fit from the root's free list
/// (ranges released pieces gave back), and only when no free range can hold
/// it, off the front of the root's unspent frontier. On any other region it
/// comes off the front and the parent becomes the remainder. `len` must be a
/// non-zero multiple of the target's protection granularity, and the region
/// must be able to serve it as one range; anything else terminates the caller
/// with `BadArg`, including a root whose free bytes are too fragmented to
/// serve it, which the caller cannot compute. A caller at its Memory or handle
/// quota gets `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded`; a full Memory slab or handle table is
/// `SosStatus.NoResource`.
///
/// There is no cut at an offset the caller chooses, for a representational
/// reason: a region is one `{base, len}` slot, and a cut from the middle would
/// leave the parent as two ranges.
@export("sos_memory_split")
public func sos_memory_split(handle: UInt, len: UInt,
                             value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, MemoryOp.Split as UInt, len, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Map: install this region in `process`'s address space, and answer with a
/// Mapping handle. Requires `MemoryRight.Map` here and `ProcessRight.Map` on
/// `process`.
///
/// Two rights on two objects, because holding bytes must not imply authority
/// over somebody's address space. `access` is a mask of `MapAccess` bits; write
/// without read, no access at all, and any bit outside the three all terminate
/// the caller. A region whose range this machine cannot grant is the same
/// fault. The target's mapping quota is capped at the protection rows its
/// domain has left, so a domain with no row left is `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded`,
/// as is a caller at its handle quota. No address space left for the range, a
/// full Mapping slab or a full handle table is `SosStatus.NoResource`.
///
/// The caller supplies no addresses. No op reads a Memory's bounds;
/// `MappingOp.Base` reads where a mapping landed.
@export("sos_memory_map")
public func sos_memory_map(handle: UInt, process: UInt, access: UInt,
                           value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, MemoryOp.Map as UInt, process, access, 0, value_out)
}

/// Carve: a sub-window of this device window, leaving the parent whole.
/// Requires `IoMemoryRight.Carve`.
///
/// `offset` is relative to the parent, so a caller names a sub-window without
/// learning an address. The parent is unchanged (MMIO is pinned and a fixed
/// region may be handed out many times), which is the difference from
/// `sos_memory_split`.
///
/// A window this machine cannot express as one protection grant terminates the
/// caller, and the rule is per-profile: one wants a naturally-aligned power of
/// two, the other a whole number of pages. So does a range past the parent's
/// end. A caller at its IoMemory or handle quota gets
/// `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded`; a full slab or handle table is
/// `SosStatus.NoResource`.
@export("sos_iomemory_carve")
public func sos_iomemory_carve(handle: UInt, offset: UInt, len: UInt,
                               value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, IoMemoryOp.Carve as UInt, offset, len, 0, value_out)
}

/// Map: install this device window in `process`'s address space. Requires
/// `IoMemoryRight.Map` here and `ProcessRight.Map` on `process`.
///
/// There is no access argument: an IoMemory can only ever produce a
/// device-attribute row, read-write, never executable. A driver therefore
/// cannot obtain a cacheable view of a register block by any spelling; the
/// guarantee is the object kind, not a check.
@export("sos_iomemory_map")
public func sos_iomemory_map(handle: UInt, process: UInt,
                             value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, IoMemoryOp.Map as UInt, process, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Unmap: take this mapping's row out of the domain it was installed in.
/// Requires `MappingRight.Unmap`.
///
/// Only a Mapping unmaps (§2.5), which is what the object is for. A mapping
/// that is already unmapped, or whose target process has died and taken its
/// whole domain with it, terminates the caller with `BadState`: the caller
/// either unmapped it or created the child.
///
/// Releasing the handle is not an unmap. Release destroys the entry and never
/// the row (§2.5: a mapping dropped without an unmap is permanent, safe but
/// leaked), so a caller that means to revoke says so.
@export("sos_mapping_unmap")
public func sos_mapping_unmap(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, MappingOp.Unmap as UInt, 0)
}

// There is no `sos_mapping_base` for `MappingOp.Base`: `Mapping.base()` reaches
// it through `call_value`, which answers a Saw caller and not a C one. The
// reason is mechanical: an `@export`ed symbol is externally visible and never
// garbage-collected, so adding one grows every user image on every
// architecture. Nothing else about the op is provisional (the number, the right
// and the kernel arm are all here).

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The pair (spec §2.1)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `ProcessOp.PipeCreate` makes a connection; the inlet posts (`Post`) or
// posts and parks for the answer (`Call`); the outlet takes (`Take`); a claim
// resolves (`Resolve`) or asks whether the answer is in (`Ready`); an
// obligation replies (`Reply`) or replies and waits (`ReplyWait`). The factory
// is on the Process object because a pipe is IPC and carries nothing
// system-shaped.

/// Make a connection, and answer with both of its ends as a record copied into
/// `buf` (spec §2.1). Requires `ProcessRight.PipeCreate`.
///
/// `cap` is the buffer's size in bytes and must be at least
/// `pipe_create_record_bytes()`. The buffer must be word-aligned and inside
/// the caller's writable memory; a buffer that is not terminates the caller,
/// because it is a mistake only the caller could have made.
///
/// The record's layout is public: word 0 the PipeInlet handle (the client end,
/// where requests flow in) and word 1 the PipeOutlet handle (the server end,
/// where they come out). It answers through the funnel rather than the value
/// register because an op returns one word and this one has two to say
/// (`sos_clock_now` is the precedent for answering in memory).
///
/// The connection belongs to the process the handle names, which is charged
/// for it; that is the caller only on its own handle. Both ends are minted into
/// the caller's table, and `buf` is the caller's. Wiring the ends outward is
/// `sos_process_give`'s job, which lets a launcher hand the inlet to one child
/// and keep or pass the outlet. The named process at its pipe quota, or the
/// caller at its handle quota, is `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded`; a full slab or
/// handle table is `SosStatus.NoResource`; a process that has ended is
/// `SosStatus.Revoked`.
@export("sos_process_pipe_create")
public func sos_process_pipe_create(handle: UInt, buf: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                    cap: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.PipeCreate as UInt, buf as UInt, cap, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Post: stage a message for the far end, and answer with the claim on its
/// reply through `value_out`. Requires `PipeInletRight.Post`.
///
/// `args` is a send record (`PipeSendRecord`): word 0 the body's address, word
/// 1 its length, then `PIPE_MSG_HANDLES` handle words, `NO_HANDLE` for an empty
/// slot. `args_bytes` must be exactly `pipe_send_record_bytes()`.
///
/// It never suspends. The length may be 0 (a data-free message is §2.1's own
/// option, and a zero-length post reads no body at all, so the address is not
/// even looked at). A length above `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` terminates the caller:
/// the maximum is published and the caller chose the number. So does a record
/// or body that is misaligned or outside this process's memory, and a handle
/// word that names nothing, lacks the universal `Transfer` right, or names a
/// kind a message cannot carry.
///
/// The handles leave the caller's table at the post: the kernel unbinds each
/// one and holds it in the exchange until the receiver takes it, so each word
/// is stale afterwards and using it, a release included, is a `BadHandle`
/// fault. A transient refusal of the send itself (`WouldBlock`, `NoResource`,
/// `QuotaExceeded`, answered before the message is staged) takes nothing: the
/// words stay the caller's. A terminal one (`PeerClosed`) releases them, which
/// destroys an object whose last handle one was.
///
/// A handle word naming an outlet of this inlet's own connection terminates
/// the caller with a `BadArg` fault, before anything is staged.
/// `sos_pipe_inlet_call` follows the same rule.
///
/// The value is a `PipeReply` handle: the client's claim on the one reply this
/// message is owed, minted into the caller's table. On a refusal nothing is
/// minted and the value means nothing.
///
/// Three refusals are statuses: `SosStatus.PeerClosed` when the far end is gone
/// (nobody can ever take it, and it is terminal since nothing un-closes),
/// `SosStatus.WouldBlock` when the ring has no free slot (transient; it clears
/// when an exchange settles), and `SosStatus.NoResource` /
/// `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded` when there is no room in the caller's table for
/// the claim.
@export("sos_pipe_inlet_post")
public func sos_pipe_inlet_post(handle: UInt, args: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                args_bytes: UInt,
                                value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, PipeInletOp.Post as UInt, args as UInt, args_bytes, 0,
                 value_out)
}

/// Call: post a message, park, and answer the reply's length through
/// `value_out`, with the reply in `reply`. Requires `PipeInletRight.Post`, the
/// same bit `sos_pipe_inlet_post` spends, because a call submits a message
/// exactly as a post does and the fusion is mechanics rather than authority.
///
/// It is the whole RPC in one trap: post, wait for the answer, copy it out. The
/// composed spelling (post, attach the claim to a Waiter, wait) is three, and
/// both remain available; a caller picks by whether it wants to multiplex.
///
/// It never answers `WouldBlock`. A full staging ring parks the caller on the
/// connection's room-to-post level and resumes it when an exchange settles,
/// which is the leg the composed send has to write by hand.
///
/// `args` is the send record `sos_pipe_inlet_post` takes, with the same checks
/// and the same rule for the handles it carries. `reply` receives a
/// delivered-message record (`PipeMsgRecord`: the packed meta, the handle
/// words, then the body). It takes no capacity of its own: it is checked
/// against `pipe_msg_record_bytes()` of the caller's real memory rather than
/// against a number the caller typed, and a reply buffer that is not writable
/// for that many bytes terminates the caller.
///
/// `SosStatus.PeerClosed` covers every way the answer can fail to arrive: the
/// server end was already gone, the server dropped the obligation, the server
/// end went while the caller was parked, or the caller's message was still
/// staged when the last outlet handle went. It is `resolve`'s own convention,
/// so the fused and composed paths report the same fact with the same word.
/// The call itself never refuses transiently: a full ring parks it and no claim
/// is minted, so no answer leaves the carried handles with the caller.
/// `PeerClosed` is terminal for them: the kernel releases them whether or not
/// the message was staged (at the call, while the caller was parked for room,
/// or after staging), and the words are stale afterwards.
///
/// The one other refusal is a reply whose handles find no room in the caller's
/// table: `SosStatus.NoResource` or `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded`, with the reply
/// and its handles lost, because a parked call cannot ask again. It comes
/// after the message was staged, so it is terminal for the carried handles as
/// well: they were consumed with the message.
///
/// No claim handle is minted, either way. A blocking client spends no table row
/// on a claim, so its in-flight depth is not bounded by `MAX_HANDLES`.
@export("sos_pipe_inlet_call")
public func sos_pipe_inlet_call(handle: UInt, args: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                args_bytes: UInt, reply: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, PipeInletOp.Call as UInt, args as UInt, args_bytes,
                 reply as UInt, value_out)
}

/// Reply-wait: discharge this obligation, then park on a Waiter, in one trap.
/// Requires `PipeRequestRight.Reply` here and `WaiterRight.Wait` on the waiter
/// the args record names: two rights on two objects, because the op does two
/// things.
///
/// `args` is a `pipe_reply_wait_record_bytes()` copy-in record
/// (`PipeReplyWaitRecord`): word 0 the Waiter handle, then the send record
/// `sos_pipe_inlet_post` takes (the reply body's address, its length and the
/// handle words). It goes through a record because the op has four things to
/// say and §5.7 gives it three (`sos_timer_arm`'s door, used for the same
/// reason). `args_bytes` must be exactly that size. As at
/// `sos_pipe_request_reply`, a handle word equal to the request handle
/// terminates the caller with a `BadArg` fault.
///
/// `record` is where the delivery lands and is the same buffer
/// `sos_waiter_wait` takes, checked against `wait_record_bytes()` of the
/// caller's real memory.
///
/// The obligation is discharged on every path (§2.1), so the caller's handle
/// word is dead whatever comes back. A Saw caller cannot reach it again,
/// because `PipeRequest.reply_wait` is a `consumes` method. The reply's
/// handles leave the caller's table when the reply lands. A `Reply`-leg
/// failure (`PeerClosed`) releases them instead, which destroys an object whose
/// last handle one was, and the words are stale afterwards. A `Wait`-leg
/// failure comes after the reply landed, so they were consumed with it.
///
/// The answer has two channels. `Ok` means the reply landed and a delivery is
/// in `record`. Any other status comes with a `ReplyWaitLeg` in `value_out`
/// saying which half produced it: `Reply` (the client had abandoned its claim;
/// the op returned without parking, so one dead client cannot stall a server
/// loop) or `Wait` (the reply landed and the wait itself broke, e.g.
/// `SosStatus.Revoked`). `Wait` is zero, which is what every waker in the
/// kernel writes anyway.
@export("sos_pipe_request_reply_wait")
public func sos_pipe_request_reply_wait(handle: UInt, args: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                        args_bytes: UInt,
                                        record: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                        value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, PipeRequestOp.ReplyWait as UInt, args as UInt,
                 args_bytes, record as UInt, value_out)
}

/// Take: copy the oldest staged message into `rec`, with the obligation to
/// reply to it. Requires `PipeOutletRight.Take`.
///
/// It never parks. `rec` receives a take record (`PipeTakeRecord`): word 0 a
/// `PipeRequest` handle minted into the caller's table, then a
/// delivered-message record (`PipeMsgRecord`: the packed meta with the length
/// and the handle kinds, the handle words, then the body). `rec_cap` is its
/// size in bytes, which must be at least `pipe_take_record_bytes()`. A smaller
/// one terminates the caller, as does a record buffer outside this process's
/// writable memory.
///
/// Two refusals are statuses: `SosStatus.WouldBlock` when nothing is staged and
/// the far end still exists, and `SosStatus.PeerClosed` when nothing is staged
/// and the far end is gone. The order is the contract: everything staged is
/// handed over first, whatever became of the sender, and only a drained ring
/// answers `PeerClosed`. Nothing un-closes, so every later take answers the
/// same. A full table or a spent handle budget is `SosStatus.NoResource` /
/// `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded`, with the message left staged for whoever takes it
/// next.
@export("sos_pipe_outlet_take")
public func sos_pipe_outlet_take(handle: UInt, rec: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                 rec_cap: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, PipeOutletOp.Take as UInt, rec as UInt, rec_cap, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Resolve: take the reply, parking until it is written, and answer its length
/// through `value_out`. Requires `PipeReplyRight.Resolve`.
///
/// It parks and never answers `WouldBlock`. A pending exchange blocks the
/// calling thread on the claim's ready level and resumes with one of the two
/// answers below; the non-blocking question is `sos_pipe_reply_ready` beside
/// it. A claim attached to a Waiter is a third door that uses neither: the
/// reply arrives in the wait record, so a claim whose answer was delivered that
/// way answers `PeerClosed` here.
///
/// `rec` receives a delivered-message record (`PipeMsgRecord`), and `cap` is
/// its size in bytes, which must be at least `pipe_msg_record_bytes()`: a
/// whole slot's record, because a caller cannot size a buffer against a length
/// it has not been told yet. A smaller one terminates the caller.
///
/// It consumes the claim on every path. A delivered reply, a
/// `SosStatus.PeerClosed` and a refused handle mint all end the exchange for
/// this caller, so the handle is destroyed with them and using the word again
/// is the ordinary `BadHandle` fault. A C caller that must not lose the handles
/// a reply carries makes table room before it resolves; a Saw caller cannot
/// reach the word at all, because the wrapper is a `consumes` method.
///
/// `SosStatus.PeerClosed` means the server side abandoned it: the obligation
/// was dropped without a reply, or the connection's server end went away before
/// the message could ever be taken.
@export("sos_pipe_reply_resolve")
public func sos_pipe_reply_resolve(handle: UInt, rec: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                   cap: UInt,
                                   value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, PipeReplyOp.Resolve as UInt, rec as UInt, cap, 0,
                 value_out)
}

/// Ready: has the answer arrived? Requires `PipeReplyRight.Wait` (the level
/// question's bit; `Resolve` stays the consuming act's).
///
/// Status-only, and it spends nothing: `0` (`SosStatus.Ok`) the reply is
/// written and a resolve will return it without parking, `SosStatus.WouldBlock`
/// not yet and the exchange is still live, `SosStatus.PeerClosed` it can never
/// arrive, at which point the holder releases the claim rather than resolving
/// it, since there is no answer to fetch. Readiness is monotonic, so asking and
/// then resolving has no check-then-act race.
@export("sos_pipe_reply_ready")
public func sos_pipe_reply_ready(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, PipeReplyOp.Ready as UInt, 0)
}

/// Reply: send a message back to the client, and discharge the obligation.
/// Requires `PipeRequestRight.Reply`.
///
/// `args` is the send record `sos_pipe_inlet_post` takes, with the same
/// checks; `args_bytes` must be exactly `pipe_send_record_bytes()`. The length
/// may be 0 (a reply that carries no data is still an answer), and a length
/// above `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` terminates the caller, as at a post. The handles
/// leave the caller's table when the reply lands; on a `PeerClosed` the kernel
/// releases them instead of delivering them, which destroys an object whose
/// last handle one was. The request handle itself cannot be one of them: a
/// record naming it terminates the caller with a `BadArg` fault.
///
/// It always consumes the request, which is what single-use means: the caller's
/// handle is destroyed whether the reply landed or not, and using the word
/// again is the ordinary `BadHandle` fault.
///
/// `SosStatus.PeerClosed` says the reply was not delivered because the client
/// abandoned its claim. It is §2.1's answer, never a silent success, and
/// ignorable with `let _ =` on the std-Channel close precedent. The obligation
/// is discharged either way.
@export("sos_pipe_request_reply")
public func sos_pipe_request_reply(handle: UInt, args: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                   args_bytes: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, PipeRequestOp.Reply as UInt, args as UInt, args_bytes,
                 0, (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Release: destroy this handle, whatever kind it names. The one spelling of
/// release in this module.
///
/// It is not `sos_<object>_release` once per kind, because release is not an op
/// on any object: it is one universal op number the kernel intercepts before it
/// has decided the kind, so there is one function here for the same reason
/// there is one op number there.
///
/// Ungated. No right is checked. A handle is a capability instance and the
/// object's identity lives in its slot, so destroying your own instance takes
/// nothing from anybody else holding one. Releasing your System handle or your
/// own Process handle is legal but doomed: you lose the authority, which is
/// what you asked for.
///
/// A word that does not resolve terminates the process. `NO_HANDLE`, a
/// malformed word, a word whose handle was already released: all are the
/// ordinary `BadHandle` fault, because using a handle you released is broken
/// code and the kernel's generations exist to diagnose it rather than tolerate
/// it. This function therefore does not return on that path, and its status
/// word is only ever `Ok`.
///
/// Typed Saw callers do not write this. In the typed layer release is dropping
/// the wrapper: that is what its `deinit` does, and this is the syscall the
/// deinit issues. It is public because the C altitude is the floor and a
/// non-Saw process needs a way to say it.
@export("sos_handle_release")
public func sos_handle_release(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, RELEASE_OP, 0)
}

/// Mint: a sibling handle onto the same object, whatever kind it names. The
/// second universal op, beside release and for the same reason: naming an
/// object twice is one act for every kind.
///
/// `keep` is a keep mask: the sibling's rights are the source's intersected
/// with it, so all-ones means "the same rights" and anything narrower
/// attenuates. The intersection makes no-amplification structural (a sibling is
/// a subset of its source by construction) and it is total, so naming a right
/// the source does not hold is a no-op rather than an error. A keep mask fails
/// closed when a kind gains a right later, where a removal mask would pass the
/// new authority through in silence. Write a complement
/// (`~(SystemRight.Transfer as UInt32) & 0xFFFF_FFFF`) when subtractive intent
/// is what you mean; the fail-open choice then reads at the call site.
///
/// It is gated on the universal `Mint` bit, which is itself maskable: a sibling
/// minted through a mask that left `Mint` out cannot mint again, so a launcher
/// stops proliferation where it says. A source without `Mint` terminates the
/// caller, as does a word that names nothing.
///
/// The sibling is independent (its own slot, generation and release) and names
/// the same object, so it is a second name rather than a second object.
@export("sos_handle_mint")
public func sos_handle_mint(handle: UInt, keep: UInt,
                            value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, MINT_OP, keep, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// The keep mask that narrows nothing: `System.mint()`'s default.
///
/// It is spelled as every bit rather than as a list of the rights that exist,
/// which is the keep mask's polarity applied to itself: a mask naming the
/// rights defined when it was written would silently start narrowing the day
/// a right is added, and "the same rights this handle has" is what the
/// no-argument spelling means.
public static ALL_RIGHTS: UInt32 = 0xFFFF_FFFF

// =============================================================================
// The owning tier's contract: what every wrapper's `deinit` is written to
// =============================================================================
//
// Every wrapper is `NoCopy`, and drop is release. Each holds one handle word
// and one hand-written `deinit` that releases it, so a wrapper going out of
// scope destroys the capability instance it owned: RAII over kernel handles,
// with the kernel's own generations backstopping it. `Mapping` is the one worth
// reading its own docstring for: dropping it releases the handle and does not
// unmap the row.
//
// The two halves must land together. A release op beside copyable wrappers is a
// stale-fault factory: copy a `Clock`, let one copy drop, and the sibling's
// next use is a `BadHandle` fault the program did nothing to deserve. `NoCopy`
// is what makes "this value owns that word" true, and what §3's no-duplicate
// rule means at this altitude.
//
// The disarm sentinel is the word field itself, which costs no second field and
// cannot get out of step with one: `NO_HANDLE` is unrepresentable as a live
// handle (it is the index byte's zero at every generation), so a field holding
// it is exactly a wrapper that has already given its word away.
//
// There is no typed `release()` method, deliberately. Early release is dropping
// the value (`let _ = move clock`), which is one concept for the reader and the
// spelling every other owning type in Saw uses. A `release()` beside a RAII
// deinit would also be a second way to reach the disarmed state, which is the
// state the sentinel exists to make unreachable from user code.
//
// The transfer-funnel contract: when a kernel op moves the word out of the
// caller's table, the sysapi funnel that owns that syscall consumes the
// wrapper, reads the word, sets the field to `NO_HANDLE` before issuing the
// syscall, and lets the disarmed value drop. Disarm-before-syscall is the
// deliberate order, so no path leaves two owners of one word. A caller error
// faults (the process ends, and teardown covers everything it held); a
// resource refusal answers a status, and a funnel that does not re-mint its
// wrapper does not hand the object back. User code never touches the sentinel.
// The `consumes` receivers (`PipeReply.resolve`, `PipeRequest.reply` and
// `reply_wait`) write no sentinel: the consuming body stands in for the
// `deinit` on that path. Generations are the backstop: a funnel bug that
// released after a transfer is a diagnosed `BadHandle` fault, never corruption.
//
// `shutdown()` never returns, so wrappers live at that moment never run their
// deinit and die in the kernel's teardown instead. That is the backstop working
// as designed, not a leak.

// =============================================================================
// The boot handle the runtime hooks print through
// =============================================================================
//
// It sits at the floor because two files need it and neither is below the
// other: `sos.system` writes it, out of `System(boot_handle:)`, and `sos.rt`
// reads it, out of the panic path's writer. A helper two seams share lives
// below both.

/// The boot handle, parked for the runtime hooks in `sos.rt`.
///
/// The runtime seams take no handle (the ABI has no room for one), so the one
/// root is given at boot is remembered here by `System.init` before anything
/// can print. It is root's own authority kept in root's own address space, not
/// ambient authority: a process that was never given the handle has nothing to
/// remember and its panics are simply silent.
///
/// `unsafe static var` rather than an `Atomic`: it is plain compound process
/// state written once at boot, and spec §7's uniprocessor argument covers it as
/// it covers the arena.
public(package) unsafe static var SYSTEM_HANDLE: UInt = 0

/// Park the boot handle. A small named `unsafe` helper is how contact with an
/// unsafe-typed value is confined, and it keeps the public
/// `System(boot_handle:)` an ordinary safe signature.
public(package) func park_system_handle(handle: UInt) unsafe {
    SYSTEM_HANDLE = handle
}
